---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { breadcrumb } from "../lib/schema";
import { site } from "../lib/site";

const title = "How I Work - Motive11";
const description = "How I work with a business: start with how it actually runs, show you a working prototype before anything gets built, let AI do the building, and stay on after it's live.";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} schema={[breadcrumb([{ name: "Home", url: site.url }, { name: "How I Work", url: `${site.url}/how-i-work` }])]}>
  <section class="hiw">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="label">How I work</span>
      <h1>You're hiring my judgment. AI does the building.</h1>
      <div class="prose-m11">
        <p class="lead">This is how I work with a business, from the first call to years after launch.</p>

        <h2>I start with the business, not the technology.</h2>
        <p>Most owners come to me asking for one thing: a new website, a client portal, a way to stop retyping the same information into three places. What they actually need is usually bigger than that, and they can't name it yet. They just know something is off.</p>
        <p>So I ask how the business runs. Who does what, where the time goes, and which job is costing the most. Then I tell you what I'd fix first and why. I'll often end up building more than you asked for, because the thing you asked for only works if a few other things are true too.</p>

        <h2>You see it before you commit to it.</h2>
        <p>For custom software, you click through a working prototype before anything gets built. You're never paying for something you've only seen described in a proposal.</p>

        <h2>AI does the building. My expertise is what you're paying for.</h2>
        <p>AI writes the software now. That's real, and it's why one person can do what used to take a team. But AI doesn't know which job in your office is costing you the most, whether a new feature will fight how your team actually works, or when to skip the custom build and use something that already exists.</p>
        <p>That comes from years of building systems for businesses, watching what works and what doesn't, and paying attention. It's pattern recognition, applied to your specific situation. When you hire me, that's what you're hiring, and the build is how it shows up.</p>

        <h2>Speed is a side effect.</h2>
        <p>Things move fast now, but that's not what I want you to remember. Speed is what happens when someone who knows what to build uses AI to build it. Without the judgment, you just get to the wrong answer faster.</p>

        <h2>I run my own business this way.</h2>
        <p>Everything I do runs through one system I built for myself: my clients, my money, my content, and the context behind all of it, in one place my AI can read and act on. I call it my AIOS. It's what I build toward for clients too, one system that actually knows your business instead of a pile of tools that don't talk to each other.</p>

        <h2>I stay on after it's live.</h2>
        <p>Launch isn't the end of the work. Several of my clients have been with me for years, and it usually grows from one project into the system the whole office runs on.</p>

        <h2>What I won't do.</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Build something you asked for that I don't think will work.</li>
          <li>Take on a business I don't think I can actually help.</li>
          <li>Hide behind jargon. If I can't explain a decision in plain language, it's the wrong decision.</li>
          <li>Disappear after launch.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="actions">
        <a class="btn" href="/fractional">Work With Me <svg class="arrow"><use href="#arrow" /></svg></a>
        <a class="btn ghost" href="/case-studies">Read case studies</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .hiw { padding-block: clamp(56px, 8vw, 104px) clamp(88px, 10vw, 128px); }
  .wrap > * { max-width: 44rem; }
  h1 { font-size: clamp(36px, 4.4vw, 58px); max-width: 14em; margin-bottom: 32px; }
  .lead { font-size: 21px; color: var(--fg); }
  .actions { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 14px; margin-top: 56px; }
  @media (max-width: 560px) { .lead { font-size: 19px; } }
</style>
